~/.config/shell/env.d/ — host-local environment
===============================================

Every ordinary, non-symlink file matching *.sh in this directory is sourced by
the configured shells: ~/.bashrc reads them before its interactivity gate,
~/.zshenv on every zsh, ~/.profile on a POSIX login shell. That deliberately
includes `ssh <host> <command>`, which reads neither ~/.bash_profile nor
~/.zprofile — a credential that exists in interactive shells alone is missing
exactly where automation runs.

This is where a secret belongs when the tool that needs it reads an environment
variable and offers no credential store of its own. A tool that has a file
store should keep using it.

The directory is provisioned; its contents never are. Nothing here is shared
between machines, backed up by this setup, or replaced by a later run.


Format
------

The files are sourced, not parsed, so a bare NAME=value assignment produces a
shell variable and not an environment variable. Export it:

    # ~/.config/shell/env.d/40-huggingface.sh
    export HF_TOKEN=hf_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

One ordinary file per service, numbered to fix the order in which they load.
Symlinks and other filesystem objects are preserved but never sourced; setup
reports them as configuration conflicts. Revoking a credential locally is `rm`
on its file.

Keep a snippet to assignments. It runs in every non-interactive shell and every
script on this machine, so a command that prints, prompts, or takes time here is
paid by all of them — and a syntax error breaks all of them.

Because that last one breaks everything at once, postflight parses each snippet
with every shell that sources it on this machine and fails on one that no
longer parses. It parses without running, and never prints what it read: a
shell quotes the line it choked on, and here that line is a credential.


Permissions
-----------

~/.config/shell and this directory are ordinary directories at 0700. No file in
this directory may carry group or other bits. setup.sh tightens anything looser
on its next run, and postflight fails on a path it cannot make private without
following or replacing a user-owned filesystem object. A snippet the owner
cannot read is private but never loads — the shells skip it — so postflight
names that too.

A mode here only holds while nothing above it can be renamed out from under it.
An account that can write to ~ or to ~/.config can move this directory aside and
leave its own in place, which is arbitrary code in every shell you start. That
is the same rule sshd enforces on ~ before it will use your keys. Postflight
reports either directory being group- or world-writable; it does not change
them, because the mode of a home directory is a decision somebody made.


Why a file and not the macOS Keychain
-------------------------------------

A process without a GUI session cannot read a Keychain secret: the Security
Server refuses with -25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed rather than prompting
where no prompt can be shown. A token kept only in the Keychain is therefore
absent from every shell reached over ssh. A file is protected at rest by
FileVault and by its mode, matching the file-based posture on Linux.


Checking that a snippet arrives
-------------------------------

Ask a non-interactive shell, which is the case that fails first:

    ssh <this host> 'printenv HF_TOKEN'
